## Proof

We analyze the TSP variant described, whose algorithm decomposes into two phases:

**Phase 1 — Precomputation on the spanning tree.** For each node we must locate the *nearest reachable node* on the spanning tree. The problem states that this lookup, per node, has been optimized to

$$T_{\text{lookup}} = O(\log N).$$

Since this lookup must be performed for every one of the $N$ nodes of the graph, the total cost of the precomputation phase is

$$T_{\text{precomp}} = N \cdot O(\log N) = O(N\log N).$$

**Phase 2 — Tour construction.** Once the "nearest reachable node on the spanning tree" information has been precomputed for every node, constructing the actual TSP tour is a single linear pass that consults the precomputed table for each node. No further tree searches are required, because every query's answer is already stored. Hence

$$T_{\text{tour}} = O(N).$$

**Combining the phases.** The overall running time is

$$T_{\text{total}} = T_{\text{precomp}} + T_{\text{tour}} = O(N\log N) + O(N) = O(N\log N),$$

since $O(N) \subset O(N\log N)$.

**Why the precomputation dominates.** Before the optimization, the per-node lookup on the spanning tree required a linear scan of the tree, costing $O(N)$ per node and $O(N^{2})$ overall — this was the bottleneck of the algorithm. Replacing each $O(N)$ lookup with an $O(\log N)$ lookup (achievable, for instance, via binary lifting / lowest-common-ancestor data structures on the spanning tree, which answer nearest-reachable-node queries in $O(\log N)$ after a one-time $O(N\log N)$ preprocessing of the tree) removes the quadratic bottleneck. The remaining work is only linear, so it is absorbed.

Therefore, with the precomputation step optimized to $O(\log N)$ per node, the time complexity of the TSP variant algorithm is

$$\boxed{O(N\log N)}.$$

